1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,720
For disk layouts with one or more file systems, 
we will look at two boot and partitioning layouts:  

2
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:20,480
the Master Boot Record (MBR), an old legacy layout 
not much used today, and UEFI together with GPT,  

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:28,240
the GUID Partition Table, where GUID 
stands for Globally Unique Identifier.

4
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:33,120
With the MBR layout, a disk 
can have different partitions,  

5
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:38,640
each containing an independent file system. 
Each partition begins with a boot block,  

6
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:43,920
which contains executable code 
that, once loaded into memory,  

7
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:50,560
can start the operating system associated with 
that partition, if the partition contains one  

8
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:59,040
OS. The blocks after the boot block depend on 
the specific file system in that partition.

9
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:04,160
At the beginning of the disk, we have the 
Master Boot Record. The MBR is the first  

10
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:11,360
sector and contains both boot code and the 
partition table. When the machine starts,  

11
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:15,200
it loads the executable in the 
MBR, which reads the partition  

12
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:22,880
table and chooses which partition to use 
to load the OS, possibly asking the user.

13
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:29,680
The partition table contains the addresses of the 
partitions. If one partition is marked as active,  

14
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:36,640
it is selected to load the operating system 
by reading that partition’s boot block.

15
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:43,120
The MBR layout has been used for decades, 
especially when rotating disks were common  

16
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:50,720
in computers and laptops. It has several 
limitations. The MBR is limited to 512 bytes,  

17
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:56,480
including the partition table, so it often 
cannot contain all the code needed to boot  

18
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:02,320
and must load more code from another 
disk section. It also supports only up  

19
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:10,880
to four primary partitions. With legacy BIOS 
booting, execution starts in 16-bit real mode,  

20
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:17,600
using 16-bit registers and instructions, without 
paging, which is no longer how modern hardware  

21
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:23,920
normally works. Finally, given the bits used 
for the partition start address and size,  

22
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:33,120
and the assumed sector size, MBR can 
only handle partitions up to about 2 TB.

23
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:39,920
Let's move now to the Unified Extensible 
Firmware Interface (UEFI), which is the common  

24
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:45,680
layout today, and which is more complex but 
offers richer features. The first block is still  

25
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:53,840
reserved for an MBR in case legacy tools expect 
it. This is called the protective MBR. It tells  

26
00:02:53,840 --> 00:03:02,160
legacy MBR tools that the disk is occupied, 
so they do not treat the GPT disk as empty.

27
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:05,360
After that, block 1 contains the GPT header,  

28
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:12,800
followed by the GPT partition-entry array. 
The actual partitions follow after that.

29
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:18,320
At the end of the disk, there is a backup copy 
of the GPT. This backup contains a backup header  

30
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:26,160
and partition entries, so the partition table 
can be recovered if the primary GPT is damaged.

31
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:31,840
One difference with MBR is that we are not 
only loading a small program that decides  

32
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:37,280
which operating system to load. Instead, 
there is a specific partition called the  

33
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:44,400
EFI System Partition, which contains a proper 
file system and executables. More concretely,  

34
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:54,000
UEFI loads .efi boot programs 
from the EFI System Partition.

